2101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1061 157023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0fe9f92-abdf-5afd-94bd-7e0416495b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1061 157024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7342dbf9-8375-507b-9fcc-03ddc4dd27a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152973 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1061 157027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "233824ca-a0f9-5e23-af03-ba0c108fc90c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152974 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1061 157028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d572a9fc-16a3-5783-b7e1-a3d5a6eab501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1064 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty:\n\n$$\nC\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1065 8004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92860310-1be6-50cf-8447-2b24a95db5e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153433 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1064 "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union. \n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since \n\n$$\n\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,\n$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{0,2,4\\}⊔\\{1,3\\} & =\\{0,2,4\\}\\cup \\{1,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1065 8005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d264ae6-080d-5ca0-be06-b6d41a5f4aa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1067 "The Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, consider the following overlapping sets: \n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.\n$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. \n\n$$\nA_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTo construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{◻,△\\}⊔\\{♡,◻\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\}\\cup \\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1065 8137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e3b30b0-95ac-5b38-a7e6-169cc3409776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153436 #edb/ref 1067 "The Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nI. $\\{3,4,5\\}$ and $\\{6,7,8\\}$\n\nII. $\\{-4,-3,-2\\}$ and $\\{2,3,4\\}$\n\nIII. $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0311da7-2e05-528f-8234-394bbb366e6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374261 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{3,4,5\\}\\cap \\{6,7,8\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{-4,-3,-2\\}\\cap \\{2,3,4\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]=\\{0\\}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374262 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce7b2aa3-4c09-5d92-b3a7-10836c641729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374263 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374263 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374263 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fbad822-a55d-54b1-a8b2-602b7b51aef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374264 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374264 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374264 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc63d538-6e7c-5e27-9165-dbb714b36dfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374265 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374265 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374265 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f0481fd-7e22-5729-91eb-6ecc92cb4c13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374266 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374266 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374266 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa0f06fd-3f41-512e-965b-e039b6730b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374267 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374267 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374267 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c66ca44-6ad4-5d8b-bfec-2c566b8ead80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\},\\,B=\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B=\\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f863cc29-74d9-5551-a15b-2146bb98a0f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374289 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\} \\\\  & \\ne \\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}\\cap \\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374290 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6c713fc4-0c22-59b0-977e-74b521261f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374291 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374291 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374291 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a75bed52-3210-5186-b0a2-6327a4e867bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374292 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 175921